                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "d31a1eff-b594-38b7-9dc9-76059c5554f4",
                        "entry_id": "46f35456-bf35-38ac-b3fe-d1462eb91349",
                        "glosses": [
                            "het grootste en belangrijkste gedeelte"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f1825429-67fc-4f3a-8475-4ff2b2d306c4",
                                "sense_id": "d31a1eff-b594-38b7-9dc9-76059c5554f4",
                                "text": "Er kwam een groot zuiveldier op de tafel van Zefod Bijsterbuil af, een grote dikke vlezige viervoeter van het rundertype, met grote waterige ogen, kleine hoorns en bijna iets van een innemende glimlach om zijn lippen. `Goedenavond,' loeide het, en het liet zich log op zijn billen zakken, `ik ben de hoofdmoot van de dagschotel. Mag ik uw aandacht vestigen op enkele delen van mijn lichaam?' Het ademde en gorgelde wat, draaide zijn achterdelen even heen en weer tot het lekker zat en staarde hen vredig aan."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "735d688e-3e3b-4c92-9597-b2d61f65b8e8",
                                "sense_id": "d31a1eff-b594-38b7-9dc9-76059c5554f4",
                                "text": "De passagiers lijken het niet op te merken. Ze staren voor zich uit, vol van hun eigen gedachten: de priester over God, Tarisio over violen en de vrouw over de baby. Je zou dus zeggen dat liefde in deze kleine, schuddende ruimte de hoofdmoot is, dat verlangen en hoop de draden zijn die deze mensen verbinden. Maar volgens de priester is zijn god de enige waarheid, volgens de vrouw is kinderroof haar enige redding en volgens Tarisio is sluw zaken doen de enige weg. Dat is geen liefde, vind ik, dat is iets heel anders."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "4bdd0688-5d31-472a-ba93-dc19a2fe352b",
                        "source_entry_id": "46f35456-bf35-38ac-b3fe-d1462eb91349",
                        "source_sense_id": "d31a1eff-b594-38b7-9dc9-76059c5554f4",
                        "source_sense_description": "het grootste en belangrijkste gedeelte",
                        "target_lang_word": "principal part",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "principal part",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "19933e5a-7f72-3b72-8451-55070d0cefd6",
                                        "entry_id": "43502517-57fb-3572-a31b-9739fcef6f53",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Any of the forms of a word which contain its stem(s) in the simplest form, or such a form that, when taken with all the other principal parts (showing various inflections), allows the entire paradigm to be derived."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "plural-normally"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3ceed56b-3cd4-4607-8ae4-23b60164d24c",
                                                "sense_id": "19933e5a-7f72-3b72-8451-55070d0cefd6",
                                                "text": "Holonyms: declension, conjugation"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1b085189-55a2-498e-bc19-c4352486c26e",
                                                "sense_id": "19933e5a-7f72-3b72-8451-55070d0cefd6",
                                                "text": "In the Verbs, I have made ſeveral Alterations from the common Form, which I hope will be found much for the eaſe and advantage of the Learner; for I have not only made them more Perſpicuous as to their ſeveral Kinds, Moods, and Tenſes, but I have alſo ſet down a Scheme of Formation, in which the Changes made of the principal Parts, i. e. Preſent, Perfect-Tenſe and firſt Supine, are diſtinguiſh’d by a ſmaller Letter, and after them the four Conjugations at length, according to the ſame Method."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "418b7031-5541-4d3f-9e1e-d30697f1063b",
                                                "sense_id": "19933e5a-7f72-3b72-8451-55070d0cefd6",
                                                "text": "There are Three Principal Parts of a Verb, from which the reſt are Form’d, the Preſent-Tenſe, Perfect-Tenſe, and the Firſt Supine."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "32eec8e7-a7b2-4206-b4c6-956842734edc",
                                                "sense_id": "19933e5a-7f72-3b72-8451-55070d0cefd6",
                                                "text": "In every complete Verb there are commonly four PRINCIPAL PARTS, viz. The Preſent of the Indicative in O, the Preterite or Perfect in I, the firſt Supine in UM, and the Preſent of the Infinitive in RE. The firſt (which is therefore called the THEME or Root of the Verb) gives Origin to the whole Verb either mediately or immediately. The Preterite, the firſt Supine, and the Preſent of the Infinitive come from it immediately, and all the reſt from them; except the Future of the Indicative in am, and the Preſent of the Subjunctive in em, which by this Scheme are alſo formed immediately from the Preſent in O."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ed876298-bf86-4382-92ea-ff89f3c1ea41",
                                                "sense_id": "19933e5a-7f72-3b72-8451-55070d0cefd6",
                                                "text": "The Principal Parts [of a verb] are the present infinitive, the imperfect indicative first person, and the passive participle."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "9678845c-2002-31ea-81e4-6073a9f86fca",
                                        "entry_id": "43502517-57fb-3572-a31b-9739fcef6f53",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Any one of however many parts of speech regarded as the most fundamental or indispensable."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete",
                                            "plural-normally"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "562bcf6a-54c0-4f34-b937-605199ca5f3a",
                                                "sense_id": "9678845c-2002-31ea-81e4-6073a9f86fca",
                                                "text": "Holonym: part of speech"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "443c6660-968e-35c8-afb9-1f220d612b53",
                                        "entry_id": "43502517-57fb-3572-a31b-9739fcef6f53",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Any one of however many parts of speech regarded as the most fundamental or indispensable.",
                                            "The noun, verb, or adverb."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete",
                                            "plural-normally",
                                            "rare"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "834fa2d9-5330-4edd-bb5c-dadd73271d57",
                                                "sense_id": "443c6660-968e-35c8-afb9-1f220d612b53",
                                                "text": "For the underſtanding of this, we muſt conceive the order of Nature. For whatſoever we find in the whole World, are either Things, Actions, or Circumſtances: The Things are known before their Actions, and Actions firſt to be conſidered, before the Circumſtances.\nHence it is that the Speech that is ordained of God in Man, to declare the Conceits of his Mind to others hath properly three principal parts, viz. Nouns to name things, Verbs to ſhew Actions, and Adverbs to note Circumſtances."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5ac00602-8bba-3e89-92c3-70e5a317b7f5",
                                        "entry_id": "43502517-57fb-3572-a31b-9739fcef6f53",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Any one of however many parts of speech regarded as the most fundamental or indispensable.",
                                            "The noun or the verb."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete",
                                            "plural-normally"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "55243d38-0798-4064-8c0e-da2cfad87273",
                                                "sense_id": "5ac00602-8bba-3e89-92c3-70e5a317b7f5",
                                                "text": "The principal parts of a ſentence are words, without which abſolutely no ſentence can be made. And are both a ſubſtantive ſignifying that, which doeth, ſuffereth, or is, and a verb ſignifying to do, to ſuffer, or to be."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0e0c029c-373e-4015-bdd6-547facb8fecf",
                                                "sense_id": "5ac00602-8bba-3e89-92c3-70e5a317b7f5",
                                                "text": "The leſs principal parts of a ſentence are words, which more fill up a ſentence, already made up of the principal parts. And are both adjectives and ſubſtantives."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "48dab8c8-10fd-4f6a-9f1b-3e45b927f0c6",
                                                "sense_id": "5ac00602-8bba-3e89-92c3-70e5a317b7f5",
                                                "text": "Nouns and Verbs are the two principal Parts of Speech, les Noms & les Verbes ſont les deux principales Parties d’Oraiſon."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "b4787f25-cb56-3410-a96a-b1468aaec6ae",
                                        "entry_id": "43502517-57fb-3572-a31b-9739fcef6f53",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A polynomial approximation of a power series, made up of monomials whose indices lie in the Newton diagram of the power series and which occur with the same coefficients as in the original power series."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
